// E0419: a law with a counterexample.
//
// A remittance file arrives once a day, and once a quarter it arrives twice. So
// `settled` drops every payment whose amount repeats one already kept, which is
// the rule somebody wrote to stop a re-sent file being banked twice. The law
// below is what they believed while writing it: that the rule only ever fires on
// a genuine re-send, so nothing is lost.
//
// It is false the first time two customers pay the same amount, and the third
// tuple drawn is already `[-1, -1]` — the generator's edge bias puts `0`, `1`
// and `-1` in a short list almost at once. So the walk reports **zero accepted
// steps**, and that number is in the output precisely so a reader can tell a
// lucky first hit from a search. Both of the shrinker's rules still run and
// reject everything they are offered:
//
//   `[]` is smaller and would falsify nothing, but it is rejected before it is
//   ever evaluated, because it leaves the `where` guard's domain — a value the
//   law never spoke about is not a smaller counterexample to it.
//
//   `[-1]` is inside the guard and simply does not falsify. The shrinker assumes
//   no monotonicity: nothing about a candidate being smaller says it is still a
//   counterexample. `[0, 0]` is smaller still and does falsify, but no single
//   element can move without leaving the counterexample, and the walk is greedy
//   over one position at a time.
//
// A reduction the walk does have to earn is in `tiers.rs`'
// `a_long_counterexample_is_visibly_reduced`; this fixture is about the
// diagnostic, not about the search.
//
// Nothing here does arithmetic on a drawn value, which is deliberate: `Int` is
// `i64` and the generator draws `i64::MAX` on every run, so a law that summed
// its inputs would report a raised evaluation rather than the refutation this
// fixture exists to produce.

fn seen(amounts: List<Int>, amount: Int) -> Bool =
  match amounts {
    [x, ..rest] -> if x == amount { true } else { seen(rest, amount) },
    _ -> false,
  }

fn settled_from(payments: List<Int>, kept: List<Int>) -> List<Int> =
  match payments {
    [p, ..rest] ->
      if seen(kept, p) { settled_from(rest, kept) } else { settled_from(rest, push(kept, p)) },
    _ -> kept,
  }

fn settled(payments: List<Int>) -> List<Int> =
  settled_from(payments, [])

law "settling a day's payments drops nothing"
  forall (payments: List<Int>) where len(payments) > 0 {
    len(settled(payments)) == len(payments)
  }

test "a re-sent file is banked once" {
  assert_eq(settled([40, 40]), [40]);
  assert_eq(settled([40, 25, 40]), [40, 25])
}
